  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records provide several relevant ingredients but not the specific result. R13 establishes the exact AlCoCrFeNi2.1 alloy and its FCC/B2 eutectic architecture, while R5 and R18 show that Al-containing CoCrFeNi-family alloys can develop chemical ordering or ordered L12/B2 phases. R6 links nanoscale order to thermally activated dislocation behavior, and R2, R8, R14, R15, and R19 provide related concepts involving planar slip, coherent interfaces, deformation-energy changes, elastic load transfer, and precipitation controlled by thermal or thermomechanical processing. However, none describes intermediate-temperature aging of as-cast AlCoCrFeNi2.1, the maximization of near-stoichiometric L12 order and jagged FCC/B2 boundaries at about 600°C, or their joint reversal at about 750°C. Thus, an expert could be directed toward studying heat-treatment-dependent ordering and interface-mediated ductility, but proposing the specific non-monotonic ductile-brittle-ductile transition and its coupled APBE/boundary-tip mechanism would still require a substantial new idea."
